p2_extra = '''    "From an implementation perspective in AWS SDK v3 and the Kinesis Producer Library (KPL), developers configure client-side partition key hashing and automated batch aggregation (`AggregationEnabled = true`). The KPL packs multiple micro-records into a single 1MB Kinesis record using protocol buffers, significantly increasing ingestion efficiency and minimizing per-record HTTP overhead. However, when using aggregation with composite partition keys, the KPL assigns an `ExplicitHashKey` directly. By computing MD5 or MurmurHash3 on the client and explicitly providing the 128-bit integer in the `ExplicitHashKey` API parameter, producers bypass server-side partition key string hashing entirely, ensuring absolute mathematical uniformity across shard hash ranges.",\n'''

p3_extra = '''    "Managing dynamic resharding at scale also requires handling the physical transient state where shard splits and merges are in flight. During the approximately thirty seconds required for Kinesis to allocate new storage nodes and update internal routing metadata, the stream status changes to `UPDATING`. Producers must be prepared to handle transient `ResourceInUseException` errors if additional resharding operations are attempted simultaneously. Furthermore, when writing infrastructure-as-code using AWS CloudFormation or Terraform, managing dynamic shard counts presents a state drift challenge: if Terraform declares `shard_count = 10`, but an autoscaler splits shards up to 20 in response to traffic, the next Terraform apply will attempt to downscale the stream back to 10 shards, causing catastrophic shard disruption. Cloud architects resolve this by using `ignore_changes = [shard_count]` in Terraform or managing shard counts via dedicated Application Auto Scaling policies.",\n'''

p4_extra = '''    "Another transformative capability of Enhanced Fan-Out is seamless integration with AWS Lambda event source mappings. When configuring an AWS Lambda function to trigger from a Kinesis stream, platform engineers can choose between standard polling and Enhanced Fan-Out. In standard mode, the Lambda poller service polls shards every second, sharing the 2MB/sec read limit with other consumers. When `StartingPosition` is set to `LATEST` and EFO is enabled with dedicated event source mapping, AWS provisions dedicated EFO pipes for the Lambda service. Lambda workers receive streaming batches via HTTP/2 push with sub-100ms latency, enabling real-time stream processing with serverless auto-scaling and zero polling infrastructure management.",\n'''

p5_extra = '''    "In large-scale enterprise deployments, streaming data architectures frequently encounter data corruption or poison pill records. If an upstream producer emits a malformed JSON payload or an incompatible schema version, downstream consumer workers may throw unhandled deserialization exceptions, crashing the worker thread. Because Kinesis processes shards sequentially, an unhandled exception causes the consumer to re-read the exact same failing record repeatedly, halting shard progress completely in an infinite retry loop known as Poison Pill Blockage. To eliminate this operational catastrophe, senior architects configure Bisect-on-Function-Error and Maximum Retry Attempts with an automated On-Failure Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) in AWS Lambda or the KCL. When an unhandled error occurs, Kinesis automatically bisects the batch into two halves and retries them separately, pinpointing the single corrupted record and isolating it to an SQS DLQ or S3 error bucket without blocking valid traffic.",
    "Finally, security and compliance governance for Kinesis streams requires end-to-end cryptographic protection. Kinesis Data Streams integrates natively with AWS Key Management Service (KMS) for server-side encryption at rest using customer managed keys (CMKs). Data payloads are encrypted before being written to the underlying storage volume, protecting sensitive personal data (PII) and financial transaction logs. In-transit encryption is enforced via mandatory TLS 1.3 endpoints. By attaching AWS IAM resource-based policies and configuring VPC interface endpoints powered by AWS PrivateLink, cloud engineers ensure that streaming telemetry traverses the private AWS global network backbone without ever being exposed to the public internet.",\n'''

